      "marks": 7,
      "answer": "$\\frac{147}{20}=7.35$ square units.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "EITHER:\n\n$\\int_{1/5}^{2}\\sqrt{5x-1}\\,dx=\\frac{2(5x-1)^{3/2}}{15}\\bigg|_{1/5}^{2}=\\frac{18}{5}=3.6$ — B1 B1 M1 A1\n\nB1 for a correct expression without division by $5$; B1 for dividing an attempted integral of $y$ by $5$. Use limits $\\frac15$ and $2$.\n\nThe normal meets the $x$-axis at $x=\\frac92=4.5$ — M1\n\nUse their equation of the normal, not the tangent.\n\nArea of the triangle $=\\frac{15}{4}=3.75$ — A1\n\nTotal area $=\\frac{18}{5}+\\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{147}{20}=7.35$ — A1\n\nOR:\n\n$\\int_{0}^{3}\\frac{y+1}{5}\\,dy=\\frac{(y+1)^{3/2}}{15}\\bigg|_{0}^{3}=\\frac{12}{5}=2.4$ — B2,1,0 M1 A1\n\nUse limits $0$ and $3$.\n\nUse their equation of the normal, not the tangent, to find a side length for the trapezium or integrate between $0$ and $3$ — M1\n\nArea of the trapezium $=\\frac12(3+1)\\times\\frac92=9$ — A1\n\nShaded area $=\\frac{39}{4}-\\frac{12}{5}=\\frac{147}{20}=7.35$ — A1\n\n[7]",
